     "data": {
      "text/plain": [
       "\"To check the stationarity of a time series data set, there are several common methods you can use. Stationarity is an important property in time series analysis as it simplifies the modeling process. Here’s a step-by-step guide on how to check for stationarity:\\n\\n### 1. Visual Inspection\\n\\n- **Plot the Time Series**: A simple way to start is by plotting the data over time.\\n  - Look for trends, seasonality, and heteroscedasticity (non-constant variance).\\n  - Stationary series typically show no clear trend or seasonal effect.\\n\\n### 2. Augmented Dickey-Fuller Test (ADF Test)\\n\\n- **Run ADF Test**: This is a statistical test that tests the null hypothesis that the time series has a unit root, indicating non-stationarity.\\n  ```python\\n  from statsmodels.tsa.stattools import adfuller\\n\\n  def check_stationarity(series):\\n      result = adfuller(series)\\n      print(f'ADF Statistic: {result[0]}')\\n      print(f'p-value: {result[1]}')\\n      for key, value in result[4].items():\\n          print(f'{key}: {value}')\\n  ```\\n\\n- **Interpretation**:\\n  - A p-value less than 0.05 typically suggests that you can reject the null hypothesis of non-stationarity.\\n  - A lower ADF statistic is also an indicator towards stationarity.\\n\\n### 3. KPSS Test\\n\\n- **Run KPSS Test**: This test checks for stationarity against the alternative hypothesis of a unit root (non-stationary).\\n  ```python\\n  from statsmodels.tsa.stattools import kpss\\n\\n  def check_stationarity_kpss(series):\\n      result = kpss(series)\\n      print(f'KPSS Statistic: {result[0]}')\\n      print('p-value : %.10f' % result[1])\\n      print('Critial Values:')\\n      for key, value in result[3].items():\\n          print(f'   {key}: {value}')\\n  ```\\n\\n- **Interpretation**:\\n  - Fail to reject the null hypothesis if the p-value is greater than the significance level (e.g., 0.1 or 0.05).\\n  - Reject the null hypothesis if the p-value is less than the significance level, indicating stationarity.\\n\\n### 4. Differencing\\n\\n- **Apply Differencing**: If your time series is non-stationary, you can apply differencing to make it stationary.\\n  ```python\\n  from statsmodels.tsa.seasonal import seasonal_decompose\\n\\n  def decompose_and_differencing(series):\\n      result = seasonal_decompose(series)\\n      print(result.trend)  # You might want to plot the trend and residual as well.\\n      return series.diff().dropna()\\n  ```\\n\\n- **Check After Differencing**: Repeat the stationarity test on the differenced data.\\n\\n### Example in Python\\n\\n```python\\nimport pandas as pd\\nfrom statsmodels.tsa.stattools import adfuller, kpss\\n\\n# Load your time series data\\ndata = pd.read_csv('your_data.csv', parse_dates=['date'], index_col='date')\\n\\n# Check for stationarity using ADF and KPSS tests\\ncheck_stationarity(data['value_column'])\\ncheck_stationarity_kpss(data['value_column'])\\n\\n# Differencing if necessary\\ndifferenced_series = data['value_column'].diff().dropna()\\ncheck_stationarity(differenced_series)\\n```\\n\\nBy following these steps, you can effectively check the stationarity of your time series data and take appropriate actions to ensure it is suitable for further analysis.\""
